#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/about-oauth/en.mdx:55
#, no-wrap
msgid "## How the OAuth flow works\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/about-oauth/en.mdx:61
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"1. Your app [redirects the user](/guides/permission-requests) to their PDS's authorization endpoint with your requested scopes\n"
"2. The user reviews the permissions and approves (or denies) access\n"
"3. The PDS redirects back to your `redirect_uri` with an authorization code\n"
"4. Your app (or the SDK) exchanges the code for access and refresh tokens\n"
"5. You use the access token to make authenticated API requests\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/about-oauth/en.mdx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid "The SDKs handle steps 3-5 automatically. Your callback endpoint just needs to call the SDK's callback handler, which completes the token exchange and returns an authenticated session. From there, you can make API calls — see [Reads and Writes](/guides/reads-and-writes).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/about-oauth/en.mdx:65
#, no-wrap
msgid "## Types of App\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/about-oauth/en.mdx:67
#, no-wrap
msgid "The *simplest* type of app is one where the OAuth session is established directly between the user's device and the PDS, as demonstrated in the [cookbook/vanillajs-oauth-web-app](https://github.com/bluesky-social/cookbook/tree/main/vanillajs-oauth-web-app#readme) example. It's simple because it doesn't require a dedicated backend service, only hosting static resources.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/about-oauth/en.mdx:69
#, no-wrap
msgid "If your app doesn't need long-lived sessions, then this approach is completely fine. But for other use cases, session lifetime will be limited because the app is a \"public client\", as opposed to a \"confidential client\". Becoming a confidential client involves establishing a secret key, bound to the client ID. If the client is publicly available and runs on the user's own device, it cannot protect a client secret. Thus, implementing a confidential client necessitates hosting some backend infrastructure, which the [cookbook/go-oauth-web-app](https://github.com/bluesky-social/cookbook/tree/main/go-oauth-web-app#readme) and [cookbook/python-oauth-web-app](https://github.com/bluesky-social/cookbook/tree/main/python-oauth-web-app#readme) examples demonstrate (both using the \"BFF\" pattern, see below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/about-oauth/en.mdx:71
#, no-wrap
msgid "See [Types of Clients](/specs/oauth#types-of-clients) for more technical details on confidential vs. public clients.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/about-oauth/en.mdx:73
#, no-wrap
msgid "There are several design strategies that can be used to \"upgrade\" a public client into a confidential client:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/about-oauth/en.mdx:77
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- [Backend For Frontend (BFF)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-oauth-browser-based-apps#name-backend-for-frontend-bff): The OAuth session is established between the app backend server and the PDS. This server-side session is associated with the user's frontend session via mechanisms such as session cookies. The frontend makes requests to the PDS by proxying them through the backend, which handles the OAuth authorization.\n"
"- [Token-Mediating Backend (TMB)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-oauth-browser-based-apps#name-token-mediating-backend): Similar to the BFF pattern, except the backend passes OAuth access tokens to the frontend once the session has been established, allowing the frontend to make direct requests to the PDS.\n"
"- [Client Assertion Backend](https://github.com/bluesky-social/proposals/tree/main/0010-client-assertion-backend): A proposed alternative to the TMB pattern with simplified server-side logic.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/about-oauth/en.mdx:79
#, no-wrap
msgid "Of these three, the BFF pattern is the currently recommended approach for building confidential-client applications. This is demonstrated in the [cookbook/go-oauth-web-app](https://github.com/bluesky-social/cookbook/tree/main/go-oauth-web-app#readme) and [cookbook/python-oauth-web-app](https://github.com/bluesky-social/cookbook/tree/main/python-oauth-web-app#readme) examples.\n"
msgstr ""

